          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding qualification measures its extent rather than changing the utterance into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.87,\"reason\":\"The expression invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond and its duties, but does not create a new promise. She acknowledges and enacts her role as Lear's daughter by affirming the love, obedience, and honour she owes him.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The avowal is a controlled, deliberately limited answer to Lear's demand, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly describes difficulty converting her inward feeling into speech.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a measured refusal of Lear's demand for competitive, hyperbolic praise. Cordelia answers while limiting her claim and contrasting it with her sisters' totalizing declarations.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, linking their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. After refusing elaborate praise, Cordelia gives this restrained avowal and defines her love by her filial bond rather than claiming limitless devotion.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia does not make a new promise. She invokes an existing daughterly bond and acknowledges duties of obedience, love, and honour. Lear does not accept this measured account as satisfactory; he urges her to amend it and then disinherits and repudiates her. Later supplied passages support the reality of her affection through grief, efforts to rescue and restore him, and her statement that the campaign is motivated by “love, dear love.”\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text identifies and is recognisable as William Shakespeare's The Tragedy of King Lear; the focal line is Cordelia's response in the opening love test.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports the utterance's avowing and bond-invoking force. It does not provide a precise contractual content beyond ordinary filial duties, and the focal words alone do not constitute a promise. The assessment relies only on the supplied passages and does not infer omitted context.\"}"
        }
      ],
